# Normal forms, substitution and instantiation, driven from the history store.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# Wherever a command expects an expression you can hand it a history position
# instead, which is how the result of one command becomes the input of the next.

# The dnf command converts a term to disjunctive normal form.

(X | Y) & Z         # store a term
(X | Y') & Z        # store another one
dnf %-1             # convert the one before the last, i.e. (X | Y) & Z
dnf %1              # same expression, addressed absolutely this time
(X | Y) & Z'        # store a third one
dnf %               # convert the last stored expression
dnf (X | Y | W) & Z # or just pass the term directly

# Note that identical results share a history slot: the two commands above that
# convert the same expression report the same position rather than storing it
# twice.

# cnf works the same way, converting to conjunctive normal form.

(X Y) | Z           # store a term
cnf %               # convert the last stored expression
cnf %-1             # the same expression, addressed relatively
cnf %1              # the first stored expression, which is already in CNF
cnf (X Y) | Z       # or pass it directly

# mnf gives minterm normal form, which is also where redundant parts of a term
# collapse:

mnf X Y | X
mnf (X | Y) & (X | Y')

# nnf pushes negation inwards. It applies to formulas, where the negation being
# moved is the formula level `!`:

nnf !(X = 0 && Y = 0)
nnf !(X = 0 || Y != 0)

# onf gives order normal form with respect to one chosen variable, so it takes
# that variable as its first argument. Its implementation is still incomplete
# and currently leaves most formulas as they are, so treat the shape of the
# command rather than its result as the thing being shown here:

onf X (X = 0)
onf X (X Y = 0 || X' Z = 0)

# Two further commands rewrite an expression rather than normalize it.

# subst replaces one subexpression by another, written as [match / replace].
# Both sides have to be of the same kind, so a term is replaced by a term and a
# formula by a formula:

subst X | Y [X / Z]
subst X | Y [X | Y / Z W]
subst X = 0 && Y = 0 [X = 0 / Z != 0]

# Several pairs can go in one command, comma separated. All pairs are applied
# simultaneously in a single pass over the input, so no pair ever rewrites
# another pair's replacement: a swap really swaps instead of collapsing both
# variables into one.

subst A | B | C | D [A / X, B / Y, C / Z]
subst X & Y [X / Y, Y / X]
subst X = 0 && Y = 0 [X = 0 / A = 0, Y = 0 / B = 0]

# Several bracket groups compose sequentially instead: each group is applied
# to the previous group's result, so here the B introduced by the first group
# IS rewritten to D by the second -- compare with the comma form above, where
# no pair ever rewrites another pair's replacement:

subst A | C [A / B] [B / D]
subst A | C [A / B, B / D]

# Substitution keeps the result well-typed: a replacement whose type conflicts
# with the matched context (say, replacing an sbf subterm by a bv[16] one, or
# mixing bitvector widths) is rejected with an error instead of being stored.
# An unannotated replacement simply adopts the matched context's type -- here
# Z becomes an sbf variable:

subst X:sbf & Y:sbf = 0 [X:sbf / Z]

# A match pattern that does not occur in the input is reported with a warning
# rather than silently returning the input unchanged. And an input that cannot
# be type-inferred at all -- like bitvector arithmetic without width
# annotations -- is matched as parsed, so unannotated patterns still
# substitute in it:

subst A + B = C [A / D]

# inst is the narrower operation: it instantiates a variable with a term.

inst X | Y [X / 1]
inst X | Y [X / Z W]
inst X Y' [Y / 0]

# inst takes the same multiple forms as subst: comma separated pairs are
# simultaneous, bracket groups compose sequentially, and the match side of
# every pair must be a variable:

inst X & Y [X / A, Y / B]
inst X & Y [X / Y, Y / X]
inst A | C [A / B] [B / D]

# Since both take history positions too, one command's result feeds the next:

(X | Y) & Z
subst % [X / W]
dnf %

quit
